00:07:54,254 --> 00:07:58,250
Story title Enhancing Climate Predictions The Impact of AI.

63
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:04,356
Artificial Intelligence, AI, is revolutionizing the way scientists approach climate modeling.

64
00:08:05,038 --> 00:08:12,644
With the ability to process vast amounts of data and recognize patterns, AI is helping to improve the accuracy and speed of climate forecasts.

65
00:08:13,358 --> 00:08:19,428
One of the ways AI is being used in climate modeling is through the development of machine learning models called emulators.

66
00:08:20,142 --> 00:08:26,820
These emulators can produce the same results as conventional models without having to crank through all the mathematical calculations.

67
00:08:27,502 --> 00:08:30,912
This allows for faster and more efficient climate projections.

68
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:41,284
In a 2023 study, climate scientist Vasily Kitsios and his colleagues developed 15 machine learning models that could emulate 15 physics-based models of the atmosphere.

69
00:08:41,934 --> 00:08:52,420
These emulators, called QuickCLIM, were trained using physical models projections of surface air temperature up to the year 2100 for two atmospheric carbon concentration pathways.

70
00:08:53,134 --> 00:08:56,868
The results closely matched those of the conventional physics-based models.

71
00:08:57,198 --> 00:09:09,316
Once trained with all three emission scenarios, QuickClim could quickly predict how global surface temperatures would change during the century under many carbon emission scenarios, about one million times faster than the conventional model could.

72
00:09:09,998 --> 00:09:16,900
This could help policymakers by exploring multiple scenarios, which would take conventional approaches simply too long to simulate.

73
00:09:17,582 --> 00:09:22,596
Another way AI is being used in climate modeling is through the development of foundation models.

74
00:09:23,278 --> 00:09:28,964
These models can later be tweaked to perform a wide range of downstream climate and weather-related tasks.

75
00:09:29,646 --> 00:09:42,692
In a 2023 paper, researchers built the first such foundation model, called Climax, which was trained on the output from five physics-based climate models that simulated the global weather and climate from 1850 to 2015.

76
00:09:43,406 --> 00:09:53,476
After this general training, the team fine-tuned Climax to perform a wide range of tasks, such as predicting the average surface temperature, daily temperature range, and rainfall worldwide.

77
00:09:54,158 --> 00:09:59,236
While these advancements in AI climate modeling are promising, there are still hurdles to overcome.

78
00:09:59,918 --> 00:10:05,668
One of the main challenges is convincing everyone that models based on machine learning are getting their projections right.

79
00:10:06,350 --> 00:10:15,108
Moreover, it will be difficult to interpret the way in which many of the AI models work, a problem known as the black box of AI, which could make it hard to trust them.

80
00:10:15,790 --> 00:10:20,228
Despite these challenges, the potential of AI in climate modeling is vast.

81
00:10:20,910 --> 00:10:29,988
With the ability to process vast amounts of data and recognize patterns, AI has the potential to significantly improve the accuracy and speed of climate forecasts.

82
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:38,788
As the field continues to advance, it is likely that AI will become an increasingly important tool in our efforts to understand and respond to climate change.

83
00:10:39,502 --> 00:10:43,076
Source www.nature.com.

84
00:10:43,406 --> 00:10:48,100
Story title Enhancing Climate Predictions with AI, Nature's Innovation.

85
00:10:48,814 --> 00:10:57,892
Artificial Intelligence, AI, is revolutionizing the way we approach climate modeling, making it faster, more energy efficient, and potentially more accurate.

86
00:10:58,574 --> 00:11:08,548
Researchers are using various machine learning strategies to improve climate forecasts, addressing the limitations of traditional methods that rely on powerful supercomputers and take weeks to run.

87
00:11:09,230 --> 00:11:14,244
These new techniques could help reduce energy costs and improve the accuracy of climate models.

88
00:11:14,958 --> 00:11:23,844
One approach involves developing machine learning models called emulators, which can mimic the results of conventional models without requiring extensive mathematical calculations.

89
00:11:24,558 --> 00:11:31,620
These emulators can predict climate patterns quickly and efficiently, allowing for the exploration of multiple carbon emission scenarios.

90
00:11:32,270 --> 00:11:38,180
This could help policymakers make informed decisions by examining a wide range of potential climate outcomes.

91
00:11:38,894 --> 00:11:44,260
Another approach involves using AI in a more fundamental way to power the core of climate models.

92
00:11:44,974 --> 00:11:50,884
These foundation models can be fine-tuned for various downstream climate and weather-related tasks.

93
00:11:51,566 --> 00:12:00,804
By identifying hidden patterns in data, foundation models can potentially outperform conventional approaches, providing more accurate predictions for climate and weather events.

94
00:12:01,486 --> 00:12:08,164
A third approach combines machine learning with physics-based models to produce hybrid models that blend the strengths of both methods.

95
00:12:08,814 --> 00:12:19,972
These hybrids can replace the parts of conventional models that perform less well, typically, the modeling of small scale, complex, and important processes like cloud formation, snow cover, and river flows.

96
00:12:20,654 --> 00:12:27,632
Hybrid models can potentially perform better than purely physics based models while being more trustworthy than models built entirely from I.

97
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:34,724
These new eye-driven techniques offer promising avenues for improving climate forecasting and guiding policy decisions.

98
00:12:35,438 --> 00:12:42,948
However, there are still challenges to overcome, including convincing stakeholders that machine learning-based models are getting their projections right.

99
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:52,548
As I continues to advance, it has the potential to significantly improve climate modeling, helping us better understand and respond to the impacts of climate change.

100
00:12:53,262 --> 00:12:56,836
Source www.nature.com.

101
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,764
Story title Unleashing the Power of Deep and Transfer Learning for Precise.

102
00:13:02,478 --> 00:13:09,348
Story title Revolutionizing Brain Tumor Diagnosis, a deep dive into deep transfer learning architectures.

103
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:16,676
In the realm of medical diagnostics, the integration of artificial intelligence and machine learning has proven to be a game changer.

104
00:13:17,358 --> 00:13:24,836
In the pursuit of early and accurate brain tumor detection, a team of researchers has delved into the potential of deep transfer learning architectures.

105
00:13:25,518 --> 00:13:32,356
This study harnesses the power of pre-trained models, fine-tuning them to elevate the accuracy of brain tumor diagnosis.

106
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:40,292
The human brain is a complex and intricate organ, responsible for various functions governed by a network of billions of neurons.

107
00:13:40,974 --> 00:13:47,556
Despite its resilient nature, the brain is susceptible to the emergence of abnormal cell growth, leading to brain tumors.

108
00:13:48,270 --> 00:13:53,604
These tumors can be classified into benign and malignant categories, with varying health impacts.

109
00:13:54,318 --> 00:14:03,012
While benign tumors grow slowly and remain localized, malignant tumors are aggressive and can spread through metastasis, posing a significant threat to human health.

110
00:14:03,726 --> 00:14:13,130
In this groundbreaking research, the team trained and validated four distinct transfer learning architectures on a dataset from the Kaggle database using five-fold cross-validation.

111
00:14:13,902 --> 00:14:22,276
These models include ResNet 152, VGG19, DenseNet 169, and MobileNet V3.

112
00:14:22,958 --> 00:14:27,524
Image enhancement techniques were applied to balance the dataset and improve performance.

113
00:14:28,334 --> 00:14:37,220
The results demonstrated that MobileNet V3 achieved the highest accuracy of 99.75%, significantly outperforming other existing methods.

114
00:14:37,934 --> 00:14:44,196
Deep transfer learning architectures have shown immense potential in revolutionizing the field of brain tumor diagnosis.

115
00:14:44,942 --> 00:14:51,588
By repurposing pre-trained models, researchers can tackle the challenges of limited labeled data in medical imaging tasks.

116
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:58,916
As the medical field evolves, transfer learning is expected to play an increasingly significant role in improving patient care.

117
00:14:59,598 --> 00:15:06,532
This study paves the way for further exploration in the application of deep transfer learning architectures in medical diagnostics.

118
00:15:07,182 --> 00:15:18,212
The potential for early and accurate brain tumor detection holds immense value for the future of healthcare, enabling more informed decisions, improved treatment plans, and ultimately, better patient outcomes.

119
00:15:18,894 --> 00:15:23,300
URL, Source, www.nature.com.

120
00:15:23,982 --> 00:15:28,516
Story title Enhancing Sustainable Plastics with Artificial Intelligence.

121
00:15:28,878 --> 00:15:35,524
Story title Revolutionizing Plastic Production, Eye-Driven Synthesis of All Natural Plastic Substitutes.

122
00:15:36,206 --> 00:15:47,972
In a groundbreaking development, scientists have harnessed the power of artificial intelligence and robotics to create all natural plastic substitutes, offering a sustainable solution to the global plastic pollution crisis.

123
00:15:48,686 --> 00:16:04,324
This innovative approach, reported in Nature Nanotechnology, combines robotics and machine learning to predict and prepare a variety of nanocomposite materials that mimic conventional plastics properties using intrinsically safe, natural building blocks, Chen Aal, 2024.

124
00:16:05,038 --> 00:16:13,092
Traditional plastic production relies on petroleum based raw materials, leading to non biodegradable products and a significant environmental impact.

125
00:16:13,806 --> 00:16:22,788
While research has established new methods to degrade petrochemical plastics, recycling remains challenging, with less than 10% of plastics being recycled globally.

126
00:16:23,502 --> 00:16:26,288
Consequently, most end up in the environment.

127
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:36,420
To address these issues, scientists have developed an integrated workflow that combines robotics and machine learning to create all natural nanomaterials with programmable properties.

128
00:16:37,102 --> 00:16:45,028
This methodology significantly accelerates the development of new composite materials, reducing the process from years to a matter of weeks or months.

129
00:16:45,710 --> 00:16:59,492
The researchers selected four natural components, cellulose nanofibers, Montmorillinite nanosheets, gelatin, and glycerol, and used an automated pipetting robot to prepare 286 nanocomposites with different ratios.

130
00:17:00,206 --> 00:17:07,492
After characterizing their properties, the team used machine learning algorithms to predict and generate new all-natural nanocomposites.

131
00:17:08,174 --> 00:17:18,436
The study demonstrates the successful preparation of various plastics, such as a transparent badge holder, file folder, shopping bag, and air pillow, using this eye-driven methodology.

132
00:17:19,118 --> 00:17:27,108
The Champion model can predict the properties of all natural nanocomposites with an average deviation of only 17% from experimental tests.

133
00:17:27,886 --> 00:17:40,452
This pioneering research significantly shortens the time for new material discovery and has the potential to be extended to various nanocomposites and advanced applications, such as wearable devices, flexible electronics, and hydrogels.

134
00:17:41,134 --> 00:17:50,436
However, there is still room for improvement, as adding more components to the mix increases computational time and requires more time-consuming characterization techniques.

135
00:17:51,118 --> 00:17:58,244
Nonetheless, the study represents a significant step towards automated discovery of composite materials with sustainability in mind.

136
00:17:58,958 --> 00:18:08,228
As the world grapples with the environmental impact of plastic pollution, this eye-driven approach offers a promising path towards sustainable, all natural plastic substitutes.

